# nfr07_no_partial_write_on_encode_fail.txtar — NFR-7: two-pass validation.
#
# GIVEN a 5-file glob where file 4 fails to encode,
# WHEN -i runs,
# THEN NO file in the glob has been modified on disk.
#
# We trigger a known encode failure: TOML cannot represent null values.
# All 5 files have TOML key k = "original". The query `.k = null` causes
# the TOML encoder to reject every file. The two-pass NFR-7 guarantee means
# no file should be modified before any write starts.
#
# However, since all files fail to encode, the abort happens during the
# encode pass — before any writes. This validates the "abort if any encode
# fails" gate.
#
# We verify no writes occurred by re-reading each file and confirming
# the value of .k is still the original string.

! nesdit -i ok1.toml ok2.toml ok3.toml bad.toml ok5.toml --query '.k = null'
! stdout .
stderr 'format\.incompatible'
stderr 'batch\.summary'

# All 5 files must still have k at the original value (not written).
# Use identity query and check the TOML output contains the expected string.
nesdit ok1.toml --query '.'
stdout 'one'
nesdit ok2.toml --query '.'
stdout 'two'
nesdit ok3.toml --query '.'
stdout 'three'
nesdit bad.toml --query '.'
stdout 'bad'
nesdit ok5.toml --query '.'
stdout 'five'

-- ok1.toml --
k = "one"
-- ok2.toml --
k = "two"
-- ok3.toml --
k = "three"
-- bad.toml --
k = "bad"
-- ok5.toml --
k = "five"
